# Fruit Into Baskets — LeetCode 904

**Pattern:** Variable-Size Sliding Window + HashMap

**Approach:** Find the longest contiguous subarray containing at most two distinct fruit types. Use a `HashMap` to track each fruit type's frequency and shrink the window whenever more than two types appear.

## Java Code
```java
class Solution {
    public int totalFruit(int[] fruits) {
        HashMap<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        int low = 0;
        int maxlen = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < fruits.length; right++) {
            map.put(fruits[right],
                    map.getOrDefault(fruits[right], 0) + 1);

            while (map.size() > 2) {
                map.put(fruits[low], map.get(fruits[low]) - 1);

                if (map.get(fruits[low]) == 0) {
                    map.remove(fruits[low]);
                }

                low++;
            }

            maxlen = Math.max(maxlen, right - low + 1);
        }

        return maxlen;
    }
}
```

## Dry Run
**Input:** `fruits = [1, 2, 1, 2, 3]`

| Window | Distinct Types | Length |
|---|---|---:|
| `[1, 2, 1, 2]` | 2 | 4 |
| `[2, 1, 2, 3]` | 3 → shrink | 3 |
| `[2, 3]` | 2 | 2 |

**Output:** `4`

## Complexity
- **Time:** \(O(n)\) — each pointer moves forward at most `n` times.
- **Space:** \(O(1)\) — the map contains at most three distinct types temporarily.

## Key Takeaways
- The problem is equivalent to finding the **longest subarray with at most 2 distinct elements**.
- `map.size() > 2` means the window is invalid.
- Shrink the window until at most two fruit types remain.
- `right - low + 1` gives the current window length.
- Update the maximum after making the window valid.
